Sea-ice is important for polar bears to hunt seals. Their main food is ringed seals. Seals are hard to catch on land, so polar bears hunt them on the ice.

Sea-ice melts in summer, but some ice remains. This ice is called the ‘ice edge’. It is where the sea meets the land. This is where polar bears hunt.

But with climate change, the ice edge is moving north. It is moving further from the land. This means polar bears need to swim further to get to the ice edge. They use the ice edge as a base to hunt from.

If a polar bear swims too far, it can get tired and drown. It is also harder to catch seals in the open water.

Sea-ice is also important for polar bears to rest on. They can sleep on the ice, and have their cubs there.

But melting sea-ice means that polar bears have less space to sleep. This could affect the amount of cubs they have.

Sea-ice is also important for polar bears to travel on. They use the ice to move around their habitat. They travel long distances in search of food and mates.

But melting sea-ice means that polar bears have less space to travel. This could affect their ability to find food and mates.

Sea-ice is also important for polar bears to protect them from predators. They use the ice as a barrier between them and other animals.

But melting sea-ice means that polar bears have less protection from predators.

All of these factors could lead to a reduction in polar bear populations.

